[Event "Jobava London vs ...d5"]

{The Jobava London (1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 + Bf4) is the aggressive cousin of the London. The knight on c3 eyes b5, and White can castle long and push the kingside pawns.}
1. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... d5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c4 and e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
2. Nc3 {[%cal Gc3b5] The Jobava: the knight eyes b5. Develops the knight to c3, where it controls e4 and eyes the pawn on d5. Black's last move put a pawn on d5, which this now pressures.}
2... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and supports the pawn on d5.}
3. Bf4 {[%cal Gf4c7] The bishop eyes c7. Develops the bishop to f4, where it controls e5.}
3... e6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e6 controls f5 and supports the pawn on d5. It also opens a line for the bishop on f8.}
 (3... Bf5 {Mirrors the bishop. Develops the bishop to f5, where it controls e4.}
4. f3 {Prepares e4 or g4. Supports the centre: the pawn on f3 controls e4 and g4.}
4... e6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e6 supports the pawn on d5 and the bishop on f5. It also opens a line for the bishop on f8.}
5. g4 {[%cal Gg4f5] Hunts the bishop. Gains space on the kingside; the pawn on g4 takes f5 and h5 away from the opponent. It attacks the bishop on f5.}
5... Bg6 {Moves the bishop out of attack to g6 and controls e4.}
6. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] Threatens h5. Gains space on the kingside; the pawn on h4 takes g5 away from the opponent.}
6... h6 {Makes room. The pawn on h6 controls g5.}
7. e3 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 supports the pawn on d4 and the bishop on f4. It also opens a line for the bishop on f1.}
)
 (3... c5 {Counters at once. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 controls b4 and eyes the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
4. e3 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 supports the pawn on d4 and the bishop on f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
4... Nc6 {Develops the knight to c6, where it controls e5 and eyes the pawn on d4.}
5. Nb5 {[%cal Gb5c7] Threatens Nc7+. The knight goes to b5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
)
4. e3 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 supports the pawn on d4 and the bishop on f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
4... Bd6 {Offers a trade. Develops the bishop to d6, where it controls e5. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5. Nb5 {[%cal Gb5d6] Wins the bishop pair. The knight goes to b5 and supports the pawn on d4. Black's last move put a bishop on d6, which this now pressures.} *

[Event "Jobava London vs King's Indian setups"]

{Against ...g6, White pushes h4.}
1. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
2. Nc3 {Jobava. Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
2... g6 {Fianchetto. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
3. Bf4 {Develops the bishop to f4, where it controls e5.}
3... Bg7 {Fianchetto. The bishop on g7 rakes the long diagonal through f6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
4. e3 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 supports the pawn on d4 and the bishop on f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
4... d6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
 (4... O-O {Castles early. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
5. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] Attacks the castled king right away. Gains space on the kingside; the pawn on h4 takes g5 away from the opponent.}
)
5. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] The kingside push. Gains space on the kingside; the pawn on h4 takes g5 away from the opponent.} *
